        "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4$, calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}(7f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(7f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7(4)+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =28+2(3-1) \\\\  & =28+4 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
